<problem>
<!-- <title>Indefinite integral &#x2D; polynomial</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to="10" numToSelect="4" withReplacement="true" exclude="0 -1 1" />
<selectFromSequence name="aa" from="4" to="5" />
<selectFromSequence name="bb" from="2" to="3" />

<function name="f" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">$a[1] *t^$aa +$a[2] *t^$bb +$a[3]*t+$a[4]</function>
<function name="polyAntiderivative" variables="t" simplify="true" symbolic="true">($a[1]/($aa+1))*t^($aa+1)+($a[2]/($bb+1))*t^($bb+1)+($a[3]/2)*t^(2)+$a[4]*t</function>
<math name="intfeval_C" simplify="numbersPreserveOrder">$$polyAntiderivative(t) +C</math>
</setup>


Evaluate the integral:<m>\int</m><m>(\displaystyle $f )dt =</m>

<answer>
  <award>$intfeval_C </award>
  <award credit="0.67" name="award_noC">$$polyAntiderivative(t)</award>
</answer>
  
<feedback condition="$award_noC">Don't forget the constant of integration.</feedback>

<solution>
<p>We first use the sum and constant product rules for integration:<m>\int</m><m>(\displaystyle $f )dt = $a[1] \int t^$aa dt + $a[2] \int t^$bb dt + $a[3] \int t dt + $a[4] \int dt</m>
</p>
<p>We then find the antiderivatives: <m>$a[1] \int t^$aa dt + $a[2] \int t^$bb dt + $a[3] \int t dt + $a[4] \int dt = $intfeval_C</m></p>

</solution>


</problem>